All cups rushed to the center of the table and hit one another with a dull sound. Ragnar drank his cool beverage in one gulp and asked, "What is your job in this city?"
"We are water carriers," thickly answered the eldest. "Lousy job, but it lets us make ends meet."
"Yeah, exhausting," sighed a very young looking lad.
"Unfortunately this is not the biggest of our troubles," answered his wiry, lanky neighbor, "now it's become dangerous to draw water."
"You don't say so." The elderly water carrier had a fit of coughing, but soon continued, "if this ain't gonna stop, we will have to find another job." A silence reigned for a few seconds before Ragnar broke it.
"What is going on here?"
"There are caves under the well we draw water from," the old man started his story in a hoarse voice. "A monster appeared there. No one knows where it came from, but it seems that it hunts by the well. A few of our friends have already disappeared."
"I heard that it can talk like a human being."
"Who told you that? A ghost from the underworld?" another laughed.
"The chamberlain even put a bounty on the beast's head," uttered the lanky guy. "After all, who of sound mind would ever go to those caves? Either you will get lost in the maze, or find yourself in the beast's jaws."
By that time, the tiring heat and wine took their effect and the whole group, including Ragnar, got substantially drunk.